---
title: "Queries"
description: "Use QQL nearest, point, hybrid, recommendation, grouped, fusion, MMR, and rerank queries."
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

`QUERY` is the universal retrieval statement. A top-level query always names a
collection with `FROM`.

## Nearest and point retrieval

{% qqlExample title="Text, vector, and point input" %}
QUERY TEXT 'vector database' FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 10;
QUERY VECTOR [0.1, 0.2, 0.3] FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 10;
QUERY POINT 42 FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 10;
QUERY POINTS (1, 2, 'point-a') FROM docs WITH PAYLOAD true;
{% /qqlExample %}

`QUERY POINT id` searches by a stored point vector. `QUERY POINTS (...)`
retrieves the listed records directly.

## Recommendation and discovery

{% qqlExample title="Recommendation" %}
QUERY RECOMMEND
POSITIVE (1, 2)
NEGATIVE (3)
STRATEGY best_score
FROM docs
USING dense
LIMIT 10;
{% /qqlExample %}

## Multi-stage retrieval

{% qqlExample title="CTE prefetch and fusion" %}
WITH
  dense_candidates AS (
    QUERY TEXT 'database internals' USING dense LIMIT 100
  ),
  sparse_candidates AS (
    QUERY TEXT 'database internals' USING bm25 LIMIT 100
  )
QUERY FUSION RRF
FROM docs
PREFETCH (dense_candidates, sparse_candidates)
LIMIT 10;
{% /qqlExample %}

A CTE may omit `FROM` and inherit the outer collection. Every prefetch name
must resolve to a CTE.

## Grouping and pagination

{% qqlExample title="Grouped results" %}
QUERY TEXT 'incident' FROM runbooks
USING dense
GROUP BY service SIZE 3
WITH PAYLOAD INCLUDE (title, service)
LIMIT 10
OFFSET 10;
{% /qqlExample %}

`OFFSET` with grouping maps to Qdrant group pagination.
